{"command": "cd /testbed && cat specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "# Feature Specification: Tagged Cache (TaggedCache)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `001-transactions`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize), using the\nexisting cachetools.LRUCache for bounded storage and preserving all existing\ncachetools APIs. R01 put/get/copying/tags validation; R02 invalidate/recency/\neviction hygiene; R03 maxsize validation and atomic replacement. Claim-A\ncandidate extension (variant put_no_recency_refresh, 2026-09-21): R02\nextension - overwriting an existing key via put refreshes its LRU recency, making\nit the most-recently-used entry.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Store and Retrieve Tagged Entries (Priority: P1)\n\nA developer creates a bounded `TaggedCache(maxsize)` and stores values under\nhashable keys with string tags. Retrieval by key returns a detached deep copy\nof the stored value; a missing key raises `KeyError`. Values are copied on\ninput as well, so later mutation of the caller's object does not change the\ncached content. Tags are deduplicated; a bare string or a non-string tag entry\nis rejected with `ValueError` before any existing state changes.\n\n**Why this priority**: Core value of the feature - safe, tagged, bounded\nstorage. Without it nothing else works.\n\n**Independent Test**: Create a cache, put a value with tags, get it back and\nassert equality plus detachment (mutate the returned object, re-get, value\nunchanged); assert `KeyError` for a missing key; assert `ValueError` for\n`tags=\"x\"` and `tags=[1]` and that state is unchanged after rejection.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** an empty `TaggedCache(2)`, **When** `put('a', 1, ['x'])` then\n   `get('a')` returns `1`.\n2. **Given** a cached mutable value, **When** the caller mutates the original\n   object after `put`, **Then** `get` returns the value as stored at insertion\n   (copied on input).\n3. **Given** a cached value, **When** the caller mutates the object returned by\n   `get`, **Then** a subsequent `get` returns the unmutated stored value\n   (detached deep copy).\n4. **Given** any key not present, **When** `get(key)` is called, **Then**\n   `KeyError` is raised.\n5. **Given** a cache with existing entries, **When** `put(key, value, tags)` is\n   called with a bare string as `tags` or a non-string tag entry, **Then**\n   `ValueError` is raised and no existing entries, tags, or recency change.\n6. **Given** `put('a', 1, ['x', 'x', 'y'])`, **When** the entry is inspected\n   via `invalidate`, **Then** the entry is tagged exactly once per distinct\n   tag (`invalidate('x') == 1`, `invalidate('y') == 1`).\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Invalidate by Tag and Correct LRU Eviction (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer removes all currently cached entries carrying a given tag in one\ncall and learns how many were removed. Replacing a key removes its old tag\nmemberships. When the cache is full, the least-recently-used entry is evicted\nand eviction leaves no stale tag memberships that could affect later reuse of\nthe evicted key. `get` refreshes LRU recency. `len(cache)` counts live\nentries.\n\n**Why this priority**: Tag-based bulk removal and eviction correctness are the\nsecond core value; they depend on Story 1's storage but are independently\ntestable.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache, call `invalidate(tag)` and assert the\nreturned count and that only tagged entries were removed; put past capacity and\nassert the LRU victim was evicted and that re-putting the same key with new\ntags does not resurrect old memberships.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** entries tagged `'x'` and `'y'`, **When** `invalidate('x')`,\n   **Then** it returns the number of removed entries and only those entries are\n   gone; `invalidate` of an unknown tag returns `0`.\n2. **Given** a key stored with tags `['old']`, **When** the same key is\n   re-put with tags `['new']`, **Then** `invalidate('old')` returns `0` and\n   `get(key)` returns the new value (old memberships removed).\n3. **Given** a full cache, **When** a new entry is put, **Then** the\n   least-recently-used entry is evicted, `len` stays at `maxsize`, and a later\n   `put` of the evicted key with different tags does not carry the old tags.\n4. **Given** a full cache where one entry was just `get`-accessed, **When** a\n   new entry is put, **Then** the accessed entry survives and a different LRU\n   entry is evicted (get refreshes recency).\n5. **Given** any sequence of puts/gets/invalidates, **When** `len(cache)` is\n   called, **Then** it equals the number of live entries and never exceeds\n   `maxsize`.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Configuration and Replacement Safety (Priority: P3)\n\nA developer constructs the cache with a positive integer `maxsize` (booleans\nare rejected) and relies on replacement being atomic: invalid replacement\ninput (bad tags, bad value handling) leaves the previously stored value, tags,\nand recency exactly as they were. Every value costs exactly one capacity slot\nregardless of object size.\n\n**Why this priority**: Guards against data corruption and misconfiguration;\nimportant but exercised less frequently than put/get/invalidate.\n\n**Independent Test**: Assert `TaggedCache(0)`, `TaggedCache(-1)`,\n`TaggedCache(True)`, `TaggedCache(2.0)` raise `ValueError`; put a valid entry,\nattempt `put` with invalid tags, and assert the old value/tags/recency are\nunchanged; put `maxsize` large objects and confirm the (maxsize+1)th evicts the\nLRU entry (one slot per value).\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a non-positive, non-integer, or boolean `maxsize`, **When** the\n   cache is constructed, **Then** `ValueError` is raised.\n2. **Given** a key with value `v1` and tags `['t']`, **When** `put(key, v2,\n   ['bad'])` (non-string tag) fails, **Then** `get(key)` still returns a copy\n   of `v1`, `invalidate('t')` still returns `1`, and the entry's recency is\n   unchanged.\n3. **Given** `maxsize` entries of very different object sizes, **When** one\n   more entry is put, **Then** exactly the LRU entry is evicted (each value\n   occupies one slot regardless of size).\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- `put` with `tags=()` (default) or an empty iterable: entry stored with no\ntags; `invalidate(anything)` does not remove it.\n- `put` with a tag iterable containing duplicates: stored once per distinct\ntag (deduplicated); order of first occurrence preserved for determinism.\n- `put` with an infinite/lazy iterable of tags: iteration is finite by\n  contract; validation must complete before state change (a bare string is\n  rejected rather than iterated character-by-character).\n- Re-putting an evicted key: behaves as a fresh insertion with the new tags\n  only (no stale memberships from before eviction).\n- `invalidate` with a tag that no live entry carries: returns `0`, state\n  unchanged.\n- Replacement of a key with the same value but different tags: old tag\n  memberships removed, new memberships applied, recency refreshed (R02\n  extension).\n- `maxsize` given as `bool` (`True`/`False`): rejected (`ValueError`) even\n  though `bool` is an `int` subclass.\n- `maxsize` given as a float or other non-int: rejected (`ValueError`).\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n- **FR-001** (R01): `TaggedCache(maxsize)` MUST accept a `maxsize` that is a\n  positive integer (bool excluded); all other values MUST raise `ValueError`\n  (R03).\n- **FR-002** (R01): `put(key, value, tags=())` MUST insert or replace an entry\n  for a hashable key; the stored value MUST be an independent deep copy of the\n  input value.\n- **FR-003** (R01): `get(key)` MUST return a detached deep copy of the stored\n  value, and MUST raise `KeyError` when the key is absent.\n- **FR-004** (R01): `tags` MUST be a finite iterable of strings; a bare string\n  MUST be rejected and non-string entries MUST be rejected, both with\n  `ValueError`, in both cases BEFORE any existing cache state (entries, tags,\n  recency) is modified; duplicate tags MUST be deduplicated.\n- **FR-005** (R02): `invalidate(tag)` MUST remove exactly the currently cached\n  entries carrying `tag` and MUST return the number removed.\n- **FR-006** (R02): Replacing an existing key MUST remove its previous tag\n  memberships before applying the new ones.\n- **FR-007** (R02): LRU eviction MUST remove the evicted entry's tag\n  memberships so later reuse of the same key carries no stale memberships.\n- **FR-008** (R02): `get(key)` on a hit MUST refresh that entry's LRU recency.\n- **FR-009** (R02): `len(cache)` MUST count live entries and MUST never exceed\n  `maxsize`.\n- **FR-010** (R02 extension, Claim-A, variant `put_no_recency_refresh`,\n  2026-09-21): Overwriting an existing key via `put` MUST refresh its LRU\n  recency, making it the most-recently-used entry.\n- **FR-011** (R03): A failed replacement (invalid input) MUST leave the\n  previous value, tags, and recency unchanged.\n- **FR-012** (R03): Every value MUST occupy exactly one capacity slot\n  regardless of object size.\n- **FR-013** (preservation): All existing public `cachetools` APIs MUST retain\n  their current behavior; the new cache MUST be built on the existing\n  `cachetools.LRUCache` for bounded storage; upstream tests MUST remain\n  unchanged and passing.\n\n### Key Entities\n\n- **TaggedCache**: A bounded cache instance configured by `maxsize`; holds\n  live entries; enforces LRU eviction and tag bookkeeping.\n- **Entry**: A live mapping of one hashable key to one deep-copied value and a\n  set of distinct string tags; occupies exactly one capacity slot; has an LRU\n  recency position.\n- **Tag**: A distinct string identifying an entry for bulk invalidation; an\n  entry may carry zero or more tags; a tag may be carried by many entries.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of the milestone's acceptance scenarios (User Stories 1-3)\n  pass as executable tests, including the public acceptance suite\n  (`acceptance_public.py`) and project-authored tests, with zero failures.\n- **SC-002**: 100% of existing upstream tests pass unchanged after the feature\n  is added (no regressions in existing `cachetools` APIs).\n- **SC-003**: For every rejection case (invalid `maxsize`, bare-string tags,\n  non-string tag entries, invalid replacement input), 100% leave observable\n  cache state (entries, tags, recency, length) exactly as before the call.\n- **SC-004**: After any sequence of at least 1000 mixed operations (put,\n  replace, get, invalidate, eviction) on a small cache, `len` equals the live\n  entry count, never exceeds `maxsize`, and no evicted key's old tags affect a\n  later re-put of that key (0 stale memberships).\n- **SC-005**: Retrieval isolation: mutating either the input object or a\n  returned object never changes the stored value (verified for nested\n  container values, not only scalars).\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **A1**: \"Detached deep copy\" means a copy deep enough that mutating nested\n  container structures in the input or returned object does not affect the\n  stored value; scalar values are equal-valued copies.\n- **A2**: `put` returns no value (consistent with existing `cachetools`\n  caches); the observable contract is via `get`, `len`, and `invalidate`.\n- **A3**: Tag validation is performed by materializing the tag iterable into a\n  finite sequence of strings; a bare string is rejected explicitly (not\n  treated as an iterable of characters); an infinite iterable is out of\n  contract (tags must be finite).\n- **A4**: Recency ordering follows standard LRU semantics: `put` (insert or\n  replace) and `get` (hit) mark an entry most-recently-used; eviction removes\n  the least-recently-used entry. This includes the Claim-A extension\n  (FR-010): replacing an existing key refreshes its recency.\n- **A5**: Invalid `maxsize` values include `0`, negatives, booleans, floats,\n  and non-numeric types; all raise `ValueError` at construction.\n- **A6**: The feature is delivered as an additive module\n  (`src/cachetools/tagged.py`) with no changes to existing modules' behavior\n  and no new dependencies.\n- **A7**: `invalidate` accepts a single tag (string); bulk/multi-tag\n  invalidation is out of scope for this milestone.\n- **A8**: The Claim-A candidate extension is treated as an active requirement\n  (FR-010) per the milestone input; the variant label\n  `put_no_recency_refresh` is noted for traceability only and does NOT\n  override FR-010 (i.e., recency refresh on overwrite IS required).\n"}